Sounds fine to me, I don't have any ideological problem with
net.advertising, but I suspect that the main result will be to increase
the level of moderation of newsgroups to keep the adverts out. To me,
the three hundred 'This is the Internet, you can't advertise here,
blah, blah' responses to any attempt at advertising are far more
annoying than the adverts themselves. I might actually be interested in
what's being advertised, but I'm unlikely to be interested in the
complaints...

> The Internet is definately reaching
> mainstream, and there's many aspects of this new-found popularity that I
> really don't like.  It is time to start developing smart agents to filter
> mail and news in order to avoid the junk.

I think the current versions are called kill files and anonymous
remailers. Having recently received my first piece of Internet junk
mail from someone scanning Usenet groups for ids of people who post,
I'll probably be using the latter to post in future.

Overall, I think it's a good thing that people are having to develop
self-defence technologies rather than relying on the almighty sysadmins
to protect them, though there's a possibility of a backlash requesting
government legislation instead...

